      "last_published": "05/13/25 12:40:12 PM (Central)",
      "risk_statement": "Given the skeletal changes that occur during spaceflight, there is a possibility that the bones of crewmembers during and after spaceflight are not as strong as they were before spaceflight and a fracture may occur postflight for activities otherwise unlikely to induce fracture prior to space flight. HSRB State of Knowledge Fracture risk (Ops) increases with likelihood of applied loads to bone > bone strength. Fracture risk (Long-Term Health (LTH)) is focused on detection of skeletal fragility. Low areal Bone Mineral Density (aBMD) has been widely used to assess skeletal fragility due to age-related bone loss. Diagnosis by aBMD is recognized by terrestrial medicine to be insufficient (requires monitoring index of Bone Quality), especially for males <50 years and pre-menopausal females. Extensive pre/post flight aBMD measurements exist for International Space Station (ISS) astronauts; exercise regimens using ARED/T2 (Advanced Resistive Exercise Device/Treadmill 2) six days/week have minimized deficits in postflight aBMDs. Notably, the minimum aBMD Permissible Outcome Limit (POL) for bone health was met before ARED/T2 were implemented on the ISS suggesting that postflight aBMD alone is not useful as a POL. Measured changes in putative biomarkers of Bone Quality, e.g., trabecular bone, whole bone structure, and estimates of bone strength, with and without pharmaceuticals, are limited to research studies. Mitigation Strategy While there are ongoing research activities on this risk, the Human Research Program currently has no plans to solicit for or pursue any new research projects in this area. The definition of fracture risk in astronauts is grounded in the ability to estimate the probability of fractures. In particular, there are two broad categories of fractures in astronauts that can be identified: 1)“atraumatic” fractures – which are due to a severely depressed level of bone strength that results in fractures with minimal to no loading to bone and 2) “traumatic” fractures – which are due to mechanical loads applied to bone that exceed the strength of bones (i.e., biomechanical capacity or competence). Atraumatic fractures are more likely to occur during long-term health (LTH ) while traumatic fractures would more likely occur during physical activities under the hazardous or mechanically-loaded conditions of planetary surfaces (OPS and after return to Earth). The inability to monitor when skeletal bones have reached a level of deconditioning that compromises the strength of bones also contributes to increased fracture risk during OPS. Because the probability of fracture influences the type and timing of mitigating countermeasure, it becomes critical to 1) fully characterize the effects of spaceflight on skeletal tissue to assess the overall condition of bones (informing estimates of bone strength) and 2) develop tools and tests to inform the Factor of Fracture Risk (where FOR=Applied Loads/Bone Strength) focusing on physical activities and/or hazards during and after spaceflight. Hence, irrespective of type or timing of fractures (OPS or LTH), a key to a Mitigation Strategy is to pursue fuller characterization of spaceflight-induced skeletal changes . It is more likely that a long-duration spaceflight of at least 100 days would induce detectable changes in bone densitometry in astronauts because it approximates the duration of one bone remodeling cycle. The measurement of areal bone mineral density (aBMD) by Dual–energy X-ray Absorptiometry (DXA) is the terrestrial clinical test for diagnosing primary osteoporosis (skeletal fragility due to age-related bone loss); this test is performed triennially in active and retired astronauts to surveil for premature fragility (relative to fragility resulting from age-related bone loss) as a risk factor for LTH fractures in astronauts. Additionally, serial DXA measurements at NASA, performed before and after long-duration spaceflights, have also been expressed as a % change from preflight aBMD. This metric is used consequently by NASA to describe the skeletal effects of spaceflight on bones, to evaluate the efficacy of in-flight countermeasures and to monitor restoration to baseline (preflight) status. Mitigation strategies have been typically evaluated by their ability to mitigate negative % changes or, alternatively stated, an ability to preserve astronauts’ bones at their preflight status. As previously mentioned, NASA could assess the risk for traumatic OPS fractures, i.e., associated with physical activities during a mission, by estimating a Factor of Risk (FOR). A FOR quantifies the ratio of applied loads to bone strength (FOR=Applied Loads/Bone Strength) where a FOR > 1 indicates fracture occurrence. Calculating a FOR involves estimating when applied loads to bones (during the performance of mission activities or with physical activities after return to Earth) might exceed the ability of bones to resist failure (i.e., fracture). A Probabilistic Risk Assessment for fractures during NASA design reference missions –which uses DXA measured-BMD as the index for bone strength, -- has been completed in support of the Integrated Medical Model. Based upon this analysis (which integrates the uncertainty and limitations of modeling tools), the probability of fracture is considered low for all Design Reference Missions and the NASA Human System Risk Board has deemed the Fracture risk for OPS to be “accepted.” Characterization of spaceflight-induced changes to bone are being expanded with new research approaches (i.e., supplementing measurement of DXA aBMD to enhance the FOR estimations of bone strength and fracture probability). Even though clinical guidelines for using novel skeletal measurements (i.e., of Bone Quality) to predict fractures are not as developed as with DXA aBMD, some skeletal changes in astronauts are notable, have been validated as predictors of fractures, and may warrant consideration for inclusion in countermeasure testing and in post-flight surveillance. In particular, there is some concern over the absence of full recovery to preflight skeletal measurements; astronauts may be predisposed to LTH fractures if deficits due to spaceflight were allowed to persist and combine with the deficits that develop due to aging. The gold standard metric for increased skeletal fragility (i.e., osteoporosis) is increased fracture incidence. However, due to limited subject numbers (<100 long-duration astronauts) and the younger-ages of astronauts (a prolonged time before fracture manifestation), the current number of LTH fractures is low. An in-depth epidemiological analysis of fracture incidence in astronauts is being updated. Recently, an analysis of fractures surveyed across all astronauts indicates that hip and lumbar spine fractures occur at higher count in the person-years following first exposure to a single long-duration mission (typically 180 days or greater) (Presented Am Soc Bone Mineral Research Sept. 2022, Austin, TX; manuscript submitted for publication Sept. 2024)). Six-month ISS missions in low Earth orbit have not resulted in a diagnosis of primary osteoporosis in returning astronauts, although the osteoporosis risk is very low in non-flying humans of similar age to astronauts. The loss of hip bone mass in ISS astronauts is not fully prevented by resistive exercise and may require additional countermeasures, verified for anti-resorptive effects, to protect the trabecular bone compartment. Lastly, it still remains to be determined if bone changes will stabilize or continue to diminish beyond 6 months.",
