      "last_published": "05/13/25 12:40:12 PM (Central)",
      "risk_statement": "Given the unique properties of lunar and other celestial bodies' dust, there is a possibility that exposure could lead to serious health effects (e.g., respiratory, cardiopulmonary, ocular or dermal harm) or to crew performance impacts during celestial body missions. HSRB State of Knowledge NASA rodent-based research results coupled with expert review has determined that for six months of episodic exposure to lunar dust the exposure standard (NASA STD 3001, Volume 2) should be 0.3 mg/m3, which is tailorable based on mission exposure duration. This standard is based on inhalation toxicity studies of lunar dust (actual Apollo 14 samples with low crystalline silica content and characterized for heavy metals) and assumes that the exposure period is episodic and limited to the time before ECLSS can remove the particles from the internal atmosphere (assumed as 8 hours post-introduction). The recommended standard protects the respiratory system from injury caused by inhaled lunar dust and is set to also be adequately protective of other relevant health endpoints. This standard recognizes that lunar dust particles will likely deposit more in the peripheral areas of the lung compared to Earth gravity, but assumes this is offset by equivalent reductions in overall dust loading; an assumption that is subject to further scientific research. This standard may be refined, or new standards created for other celestial body dust exposure-based dust composition and mission profile. Lunar polar ice/subsurface regolith may contain volatiles of toxicological concern (e.g., mercury, ammonia, hydrogen sulfide) that are unlikely to be retained in dust on suits, etc., but will need to be assessed to ensure appropriate containment if samples are being stored in a habitable environment. Weight of evidence does not suggest a likely allergen challenge, although this potential is difficult to rule out entirely. Mitigation Strategy The impact of exposure to dust from extraterrestrial sources (celestial dusts) could lead to respiratory, cardiac, ocular, or dermal harm during exploration surface missions, resulting in immediate or long-term health effects. NASA needs to sufficiently characterize the consequences of exposure to these dusts so that vehicles and habitats are designed to maintain concentrations of airborne dust within safe limits while future operations planning minimizes the dust impacts on human health and performance. NASA rodent based research results coupled with expert review have established a permissible exposure limit (PEL) for lunar dust that has been converted into a NASA standard. This lunar dust NASA standard is being used to develop engineering controls for lunar surface missions that keep astronaut exposures below the PEL. Current research is focused on determining the allergenicity of lunar dust exposure and future work will be performed on Mars surface samples once acquired.",
