      "hrp_element": "Human Health Countermeasures (HHC)",
      "last_published": "05/13/25 12:40:12 PM (Central)",
      "risk_statement": "Given the high-performance physiological and functional demands of operating in a self-contained EVA or training suit in various gravity fields and system environments, there is a possibility that crew injury and compromised physiological and functional performance may occur. HSRB State of Knowledge NASA-STD-3001: NASA Spaceflight Human-System Standard Vol 1: Crew Health and Vol 2: Human Factors, Habitability, and Environmental Health and NASA/SP-2010-3407, Human Integration Design Handbook (HIDH): suit life support and basic functionality; HIDH includes observed metabolic rates and human factors; no suit-induced operational injury prevention or operations guidance. Training injury data available, but injury mechanisms and associated individual risk factors (sex, anthropometry, fitness, etc.) are not yet understood. Microgravity EVA performance is characterized, but the physiological and functional demands of planetary EVAs are not understood, outside of a limited number of Lunar and Mars sites, where humans and robotic landers have been. Mitigation Strategy EVA preparedness and performance must be characterized as functions of gravitational field transitions and associated sensorimotor adaptations, as well as physical and cognitive fitness during operations requiring high operational tempos of surface EVAs. Adequate characterization will allow for development of countermeasures to combat sensorimotor deficits and physiologic decrements, initially by establishing techniques to monitor and promote general crew readiness. Additionally, the risks of DCS and hypoxia must be characterized to ensure that effects from living in a hypobaric, hypoxic environment (to enable rapid, frequent EVAs) do not exacerbate any negative health or performance-related effects. Characterization of human performance capabilities, as well as validation and operationalization of prebreathe protocols, in lower pressure habitats and elevated pressure spacesuits will serve to sufficiently mitigate risks to individual crewmembers and mission objectives. An overarching approach for mitigation of the EVA risk is outlined in the most recent version of the Integrated EVA Human Research Plan (currently here: https://www.nasa.gov/sites/default/files/atoms/files/tp-20205007604.pdf) .”",
