      "last_published": "05/13/25 12:40:12 PM (Central)",
      "risk_statement": "Given that the conditions of space missions may lead to inadequate functioning within a team (inadequate cooperation, coordination, communication and/or psychosocial adaptation), which includes flight crew and ground support, there is a possibility that performance and behavioral health decrements will occur. HSRB State of Knowledge There is evidence of occasional conflict within crews preflight and in-flight, within and across International Space Station (ISS) partner subgroups. ISS crews reported concerns with crew readiness to engage in effective teamwork during off-nominal events. In off-nominal events identified as requiring \"exceptional\" teamwork, crews have responded incorrectly. There is evidence of significant impacts on individual and team performance, stress, and cohesion caused by communication delays and coordination inefficiencies between crew and the Mission Control Center (MCC). There is evidence of occasional team preparedness deficiencies during preflight and in-flight due to switching between individual and team activities, and team and technical skill decay. Selection has successfully incorporated individual-level assessments of team skills and capabilities, but crew composition is not data-driven. Team training and performance is not systematically monitored nor evaluated, except confidentially by operational psychologists. Commercialization and private astronaut crew integration with professional astronauts introduce risk to NASA astronauts, but the level or risk is not well-quantified. Mitigation Strategy The risk mitigation strategy of the HFBP Element follows the larger strategy of the HRP “from Evidence to Products” principle, while being driven by Operations. Specific tasks are defined to address the gaps and provide clear, specific deliverables, ensuring an evidence- and operationally-based end item. To ensure relevancy, stakeholders are engaged in the process at the beginning. Findings from HFBP research inform operations, standards, and updates to the evidence base for the HFBP Risks. The mitigation strategy for this area of research is to develop an empirically-based approach to identify the most relevant and serious threats to task performance, teamwork, and psycho-social performance within the team context. Utilizing a common set of criteria will allow for the systematic review and analysis of knowledge as well as current practices/policies when implementing Behavioral Health and Performance (BHP) operations. Once these threats have been identified, researchers will determine the most effective ways to monitor and measure these threats as well as identify the optimal and timely countermeasures to mitigate risk. Countermeasures integrate individual- and team-level psychological processes with human factors design relative to habitability, training, and interactions with robotics, computers, and automated systems to manage risk from multiple perspectives. Notably, specific factors have been identified as highly relevant to long-duration space missions (e.g., crew composition, communication, autonomy, team skills). Thus, these gaps will focus specifically on understanding the true impact of these factors on long duration missions and mitigating the risk through countermeasure development.",
